WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:02.775
COLT MCARLIS: In this episode
of Cloud Performance Atlas,

00:00:02.775 --> 00:00:05.820
I help some bacon lovers
profile Kubernetes Startup Time.

00:00:05.820 --> 00:00:08.342
We'll their performance end
up with the perfect crunch?

00:00:08.342 --> 00:00:09.300
Stay tuned to find out.

00:00:11.792 --> 00:00:13.500
After my work with
the foodie picks team,

00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:15.210
another food based
company approach

00:00:15.210 --> 00:00:18.090
me to help with more cold
boot performance problems.

00:00:18.090 --> 00:00:20.520
Bacon Live is a mobile
network for chefs,

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:23.320
where you can watch live video
feeds of people cooking food,

00:00:23.320 --> 00:00:24.590
annotations included.

00:00:24.590 --> 00:00:26.880
The back end for Bacon
Live was using Kubernetes,

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:29.460
which is a great tool to help
you manage, contain, and deploy

00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:32.640
images to your various instances
in a seamless and powerful

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:33.330
manner.

00:00:33.330 --> 00:00:34.950
Sadly though,
reporting back details

00:00:34.950 --> 00:00:37.860
on cold boot performance was
not something it did easily.

00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:39.900
So before we could solve
Bacon Live's issues,

00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:43.080
we had to figure out how to
profile pod startup time.

00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:45.420
After digging around,
neither the cloud console,

00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:48.420
stack driver or heapster
surfaced any direct numbers

00:00:48.420 --> 00:00:50.490
with respect to the
boot time for pods.

00:00:50.490 --> 00:00:53.160
There was, however, a single
place where some startup time

00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:56.130
information was being surfaced,
in the Kub Control Get Pod

00:00:56.130 --> 00:00:56.635
method.

00:00:56.635 --> 00:00:58.260
It turns out that
calling this function

00:00:58.260 --> 00:01:01.500
will return a JSON string with
some time-stamped information

00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:03.690
relating to the
pod startup phases.

00:01:03.690 --> 00:01:05.760
Here you can see
that the pod status

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:08.586
emits the scheduled,
initialized, and ready phases,

00:01:08.586 --> 00:01:09.960
each with a time
stamp that tells

00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:11.730
us relatively when it occurred.

00:01:11.730 --> 00:01:14.370
So for this example, you can see
that it took about one second

00:01:14.370 --> 00:01:15.991
to go from initialized to ready.

00:01:15.991 --> 00:01:18.240
So we can at least get some
information about how long

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:19.830
things take to boot.

00:01:19.830 --> 00:01:22.740
Now to get a sense of the
average startup performance

00:01:22.740 --> 00:01:25.350
and what it looks like, we
need to create a loop, where

00:01:25.350 --> 00:01:28.170
we kill the current pods,
let new ones boot up,

00:01:28.170 --> 00:01:30.750
read their time-stamp
data, and then repeat.

00:01:30.750 --> 00:01:32.640
Do that about 100 times
or so, and we end up

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:34.765
with something that looks
like this, which shows us

00:01:34.765 --> 00:01:37.230
that for a blank pod, it
takes about three milliseconds

00:01:37.230 --> 00:01:40.230
to boot it up, which by the way,
is a lot faster than booting

00:01:40.230 --> 00:01:42.100
a compute engine instance.

00:01:42.100 --> 00:01:45.240
And the reason for that is that
when a Kubernetes pod boots up,

00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:47.370
it's not starting a
brand new VM each time.

00:01:47.370 --> 00:01:49.560
It's just booting containers.

00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:53.070
See Kubernetes pods are made
up of a series of containers,

00:01:53.070 --> 00:01:55.470
which provide endpoints and
services for the instance

00:01:55.470 --> 00:01:56.190
itself.

00:01:56.190 --> 00:01:59.520
A pod is considered ready
once all the containers have

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:02.220
been initialized and
their health checks pass.

00:02:02.220 --> 00:02:04.440
Now to help facilitate
proper boot up,

00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:06.450
the initialization process
breaks up containers

00:02:06.450 --> 00:02:07.900
into two main groups.

00:02:07.900 --> 00:02:10.440
The first is the Init Group,
which initializes specific

00:02:10.440 --> 00:02:11.790
containers in a linear .

00:02:11.790 --> 00:02:12.510
Order

00:02:12.510 --> 00:02:14.130
The second is the
Parallel Group,

00:02:14.130 --> 00:02:17.400
which initializes the remaining
containers all at once.

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:20.220
The setup has some
interesting quirks and chances

00:02:20.220 --> 00:02:21.480
to be problematic.

00:02:21.480 --> 00:02:23.880
For instance, if a single
container during the init phase

00:02:23.880 --> 00:02:27.284
takes a long time, it will block
loading of everything else.

00:02:27.284 --> 00:02:29.700
So to understand where a pod
boot up performance is going,

00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:31.800
we need to be able to
time the specific startup

00:02:31.800 --> 00:02:34.930
duration of each
container in each group.

00:02:34.930 --> 00:02:36.714
So let's start with
the init containers.

00:02:36.714 --> 00:02:38.880
After a bit of digging, we
found that the Kubernetes

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:41.850
get pod command will return
the status of the target pod

00:02:41.850 --> 00:02:43.144
during boot up time.

00:02:43.144 --> 00:02:45.060
And when you have the
init containers defined,

00:02:45.060 --> 00:02:49.170
the Status field will list the
status of the init containers.

00:02:49.170 --> 00:02:52.200
For example, a status
of init 16 indicates

00:02:52.200 --> 00:02:55.930
that one of six init containers
has completed successfully,

00:02:55.930 --> 00:02:59.430
which means that we can pull the
pod every 10 milliseconds or so

00:02:59.430 --> 00:03:02.244
and get a sense of how long
each stage is taking to boot up.

00:03:02.244 --> 00:03:03.660
When we graph that
information, it

00:03:03.660 --> 00:03:06.720
becomes really easy to see that
for this example, the database

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:08.850
initialization and stats
frameworks are taking

00:03:08.850 --> 00:03:10.230
the longest time to boot.

00:03:10.230 --> 00:03:11.940
Now timing the
parallel containers

00:03:11.940 --> 00:03:15.120
is much trickier, since there's
no time stamp or extra data

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:17.250
emitted when a container
makes a transition

00:03:17.250 --> 00:03:19.650
from initialized to being
ready to handle requests.

00:03:19.650 --> 00:03:21.720
So in order for this
information to be surfaced,

00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:24.480
we have to probe our endpoints,
much like health checks

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:26.190
or readiness probes would.

00:03:26.190 --> 00:03:28.950
These systems sit in a tight
loop and touch the end points

00:03:28.950 --> 00:03:30.907
until a specific
response is received,

00:03:30.907 --> 00:03:33.240
which lets the load balancer
know that this container is

00:03:33.240 --> 00:03:34.750
ready to handle traffic.

00:03:34.750 --> 00:03:37.110
So for our set up, we
modify each container

00:03:37.110 --> 00:03:38.730
to expose a new
entry point, which

00:03:38.730 --> 00:03:40.830
returns the string "success."

00:03:40.830 --> 00:03:42.750
Repeatedly, we're
pulling this end point

00:03:42.750 --> 00:03:45.720
from an external location,
yields three different states.

00:03:45.720 --> 00:03:48.330
First is error, which
means that the service is

00:03:48.330 --> 00:03:49.980
not up or running yet.

00:03:49.980 --> 00:03:52.980
Second, is code of 400
or 500 series, which

00:03:52.980 --> 00:03:54.450
means that the
service is booting,

00:03:54.450 --> 00:03:56.220
but the end point is
not available yet.

00:03:56.220 --> 00:03:57.900
Or finally, "success."

00:03:57.900 --> 00:04:00.150
If we pull the end points
every 10 milliseconds or so,

00:04:00.150 --> 00:04:02.160
we can figure out how
long the container sits

00:04:02.160 --> 00:04:05.700
in each of these states and
chart it as a pretty graph.

00:04:05.700 --> 00:04:07.260
So armed with this
information, we

00:04:07.260 --> 00:04:10.080
could finally start looking at
what was causing Bacon Live's

00:04:10.080 --> 00:04:11.380
performance problems.

00:04:11.380 --> 00:04:13.189
But for this episode,
we're out of time.

00:04:13.189 --> 00:04:14.730
If you'd like to
know more, check out

00:04:14.730 --> 00:04:16.920
the rest of the Cloud
Performance Atlas content.

00:04:16.920 --> 00:04:18.990
And remember, when it
comes to performance,

00:04:18.990 --> 00:04:20.610
every millisecond counts.

00:04:20.610 --> 00:04:26.060
[MUSIC PLAYING]

